{
  "extends": "./tsconfig.json",

  /*
   * 🔴 **The BUILD compiles this project only, and `include` is narrowed HERE
   * rather than in `tsconfig.json`.**
   *
   * `tsconfig.json` includes `../shared/**` so `npm run typecheck` and Jest see
   * the shared geometry. Inheriting that here made TypeScript infer the emit
   * root from the widest common ancestor — the repository root — so the build
   * wrote `dist/optioniaWooCommerceBackend/src/main.js` while `start:prod` runs
   * `node dist/main.js`. The server could not start, and 1,712 unit tests,
   * 1,279 e2e, lint, typecheck and 31 gates were all green, because none of them
   * run the built artefact.
   *
   * ⚠️ **It also put six `.spec.js` files in the production build**: the
   * inherited `**/*spec.ts` exclude stopped matching once the shared tree joined
   * the program at a different path depth.
   *
   * ✅ **IT HAPPENED, 2026-10-05 — and `include` did NOT need changing.**
   * `design/render-worker.service.ts` imports `@shared/design/design-scene`,
   * because a print file must come from the SAME serializer the dashboard
   * preview and the storefront draw with, or it matches the preview a merchant
   * approved only by accident. (Mirroring was right for `design-fonts.ts`,
   * which needed eight strings; it is wrong for a renderer.)
   *
   * 🔴 **TypeScript followed the import on its own.** The shared sources joined
   * the program because something under `src/**` references them — not because
   * this list was widened — and the emit root moved to the repository root
   * exactly as predicted. `start:prod` was repointed to
   * `dist/optioniaWooCommerceBackend/src/main.js` rather than fighting that,
   * because `check-build-output.sh` reads the entry point FROM the script
   * deliberately, so it protects whatever the script says.
   *
   * ⚠️ **Verified rather than assumed**: the compiled worker was loaded in
   * node and its import resolved — TypeScript rewrites the alias to
   * `../../../shared/design/design-scene`, which lands on the emitted
   * `dist/shared/`. An alias surviving compilation but not runtime would be a
   * server that starts and throws on the first render.
   *
   * 📌 **`**/*spec.ts` still excluded**, which is what kept six `.spec.js`
   * files out of the artefact the last time this root moved.
   */
  "include": ["src/**/*"],
  /*
   * 🔴 **`../shared/**/*.spec.ts` by PATH, because the glob alone did not
   * reach it.** `exclude` filters what `include` matched, and the shared tree
   * arrives through the import graph instead — so `**/*spec.ts` left 91 spec
   * files in the production build, which is the precise defect the comment
   * above warned about when the root last moved. Caught by
   * `check-build-output.sh`, not by review.
   */
  "exclude": [
    "node_modules",
    "test",
    "dist",
    "**/*spec.ts",
    "../shared/**/*.spec.ts"
  ]
}
